It happens fast, the way disasters and rescues do.

You drive the crowbar and the second slab climbs — your shoulders burning, Toma moaning and pulling with both arms — and his legs come free with a scrape of torn boot-leather. Not crushed, by the Father's mercy: bruised black and bloodied, but his. He sags against you, shaking, and you shout for Veylin to haul.

And the shadow steps closer.

You feel it before anything: warmth leaving your back like a door opened on winter. You spin, shield up — and the thing is on you, longer arms than any man, a blackness of a hand that drinks the lantern's circle as it comes. It rakes at you and you catch the blow on your shield, the wood going cold under your arm where it strikes, cold enough to burn.

Then the gap behind you fills with grey beard. Veylin — haul line forgotten, having wedged his own broad carcass through the fall on the sound of stone — plants his feet beside you, will not look anywhere but your boots, and puts out one hand in the hammer-sign. "The Father's fire, by His forge," he says, calm as ever, "the wholesome flame on the faithless dark." Light falls out of his palm like a struck spark from the anvil — clean, white, warm — and takes the shadow full in its faceless place.

It doesn't scream. It comes apart. Where the light touches it, the thing thins, and shivers, and blows away like ash off a bloom, and where it stood there is just the seam's glow on the wall — which flares once, hard, angry, and dims, like a sleeper disturbed. Farther down the drift, the two remote lights go out. Not fade — go out, all at once, like eyes closing.

In the ringing quiet after, Veylin lowers his hand, and recites, as though nothing at all had happened: "'The faithful anvil fears no spark,' it says in the fifth hymn. I always liked that verse."

Toma is out of the fall, alive, whole enough. His legs are colors no legs should be, and he is shivering worse than Odo did, but he grips your hand and says: "You're both real. He said you weren't. Merrick said — after the light walked behind him — he said none of you were real anymore."

The rope hangs at your back, through the gap, toward the way out. Below, past the drift's rubble, the pale glow of the seam still lies along the far wall; and past it, far down, is the deep stair where Merrick went with his brass sample-boxes, and no one has heard him in two days.

