Toma thinks while the brothers lash the sledge, and thinking visibly costs him. "Once, master. One time I saw him, up by the assay office behind the counting-house — old assay office, shut since the north workings were. Grey glove, fine stitching, not a working man's leather by a mile. Merrick met him in the dark and I only came around the corner in time to see the coin pass and the glove snap shut." His eyes go distant, straining after it. "There was a little mark on the clasp of it. Silver. A circle... with scales inside the circle. Like for weighing." He shakes his head slowly. "Merrick called him the assayer, but joking-like, the way you'd joke about a thing that frightened you. I never heard his voice. He never spoke when I was near. Smooth glove, master. Smooth like it had never gripped a tool."

You fix that mark in your memory — circle and scales — and pass it to the lay brothers word for word for Hessa's ear, along with all the rest. "She'll have it before the council has their mortar," the elder of them says grimly, and the sledge lamps climb the lane.

You check the lantern by your own hand: good for the better part of two hours yet, and no arguing with your own thrift. Veylin takes his ten breaths, adds six more for the Father's sake, and then you go down together.

The mine swallows you a second time easier than the first — the same rails, the same bend, the soot warning on your left this time coming down past the third gallery like a burnt warning beacon. The gap in the collapse takes you both, Veylin grunting mail past the stone. And on the far side the drift lies still and cold, the seam's glow lying along the far wall like snowfield light, dimmer now, or the same, and hard to judge when you don't look at it.

But you follow the glow without looking at it, along the rubble slope to where the drift's floor drops, and there, cut descending into the rock at the drift's end, is the deep stair: a hundred worn steps, old water-dark, rail-tunnels below breathing past you faint and cold on the poreless air. Your lantern bpockets the dark ten feet down and loses the rest.

Halfway measures of stone tell you where each of you is. Above you the mountain. Below you — a pump chamber the size of a chapel, if Toma's memory held, and water-sound in it, and beyond that, the whole blind dark of the old lower workings, and Merrick with his brass boxes.

Your lantern reaches below, and following it, your boots. The steps of the stair are hollowed in the middle from generations of trammer traffic. And on the seventeenth step down, the lantern-light finds the brass — one of Merrick's sample-boxes, snapped shut, lying on the stair, and none of Merrick's blood. And somewhere below, faintly, rising through the drip and general breathing of the stone, a sound of slow metal, as of something small and brass being placed, with great care, next to something else. As if a man were still arranging them neatly.

